    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d8">

                <pb facs="0103" n="8" sameAs="frus:frus1936v04/pg8"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/605: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the American Delegation</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-01-10T01:00:00+00:00">January 10, 1936—1
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 9—9:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>40. Supplementing my 39, Jan. 9, 7 p.m.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d8-f1"/> Eden told
                    me it was his opinion that the so-called negotiations between China and Japan
                    for a non-aggression pact were not as serious a matter as Craigie thought and
                    that in a talk with the Chinese Ambassador he gathered the impression that the
                    Chinese are discussing this idea in order to play for time but without any
                    serious intention of consummating such a pact.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
